---
/**
 * HV3 . THE CURSOR
 *
 * 2026-09-27. HOVER A WORK AND IT ANSWERS: format, platform, role, year.
 * Eight drafts, ab-hv1 through ab-hv8. Everything else on the page is
 * exactly as it is live.
 *
 * THE FACTS COME FROM THE MARKDOWN, through src/lib/wkinfo.ts, which all
 * eight drafts import so that a difference between them is a difference of
 * DESIGN and never of data. Read the header of that file: two works have no
 * written Format row and fall back to `category`, four have no platform --
 * three of which carry Ugam's own "Channels: Social-first" -- and Book My
 * Yamraj has neither and returns THREE rows rather than four. Every draft
 * is built to take three.
 *
 * THE LABELS ARE NOT MONOSPACE, and that is deliberate. They are declared
 * `var(--mono)`, the token every label on this page uses -- but this file
 * redefines it: `:root .rg { --mono: var(--display); }`, the 2026-09-21
 * re-face that took the running head, the corner labels and the Scroll cue
 * off JetBrains Mono. Declaring the token rather than a family means these
 * labels went with them, and will follow the page again if the licensed
 * Neue Haas is ever installed. Measured here, it is not: the named "Neue
 * Haas Grotesk Display" sets "Selected works" at 608.15px, exactly what a
 * deliberately invented family name gives, while "Helvetica Neue" gives
 * 688.80 -- so the stack falls through and every figure below is measured
 * off Helvetica Neue.
 *
 * THE FRAME, MEASURED AT 1280x800 mid-section, because every placement
 * below is an answer to it. .wk__pin is the viewport, 1280x800, and its
 * mask takes the top and bottom 9% to transparent, so the usable band is
 * y 72 to 728. The centred work occupies x 212.2 to 769.1, y 243.4 to
 * 556.7. The readout sits at x 280 to 1000, y 584 to 713.6. The running
 * head is fixed at the bottom LEFT, x 77, y 760. That leaves exactly four
 * clear places to put anything: the band above the card (171px tall, full
 * width), the picture itself, the four corners of the frame, and the
 * readout's own slot -- and the eight drafts divide them between them.
 *
 * -- THIS ONE: THE CURSOR ----------------------------------------
 *
 * THE READING IS IN THE HAND. The block is carried by the pointer rather
 * than placed on the frame: it sits 18px to the right of the cursor and
 * travels with it across the picture. The only draft in the set where
 * where-you-are-looking and where-the-words-are are the same place.
 *
 * AND THE ONE IDEA THAT MAKES IT EDITORIAL RATHER THAN A TOOLTIP: X IS
 * CONTINUOUS, Y IS NOT. The block follows the cursor's x to the pixel, but
 * its y SNAPS TO A 24px BASELINE GRID. Move the hand and the words slide
 * smoothly sideways while stepping vertically from line to line, exactly as
 * type does on a page. The hand is analogue; the setting is not. It is the
 * cheapest possible statement of the whole design system and it costs one
 * Math.round.
 *
 * NO LERP, NO SPRING, NO SCALE-UP. Those are the moves that make a
 * cursor-follower feel like a toy, and the brief asks for responsive rather
 * than decorative. The position is written synchronously in the pointermove
 * handler -- two custom properties, which is what src/scripts/frames.ts
 * already does for the frame scrub on the same event and for the same
 * reason -- so the block is never behind the hand. The ONLY eased thing
 * here is the 110ms fade on arrival.
 *
 * IT FLIPS AT THE EDGE. Within 200px of the frame's right margin the block
 * moves to the pointer's left and sets flush right, so it can never be
 * pushed out of the frame or clipped by .wk__pin's overflow.
 *
 * THE TICK IS THE ONLY DECORATION AND IT IS LOAD-BEARING: a 12px hairline
 * from the cursor to the block's first character, so that the reading is
 * visibly ATTACHED to the point rather than floating near it. It flips with
 * the block.
 *
 * SMALL, BECAUSE IT IS UNDER YOUR HAND. Values at 12px and labels at 9px --
 * the smallest setting in this set. Anything larger covers the picture it
 * is describing, and at 18px from the cursor it is already at reading
 * distance.
 *
 * <- HONEST LIMIT. CSS transitions freeze at their start value in the
 *   preview pane (HANDOFF 6), so every arrival below is verified the ways
 *   that need no frames: declared durations, delays and timing functions
 *   read off the computed style, and the same interpolation re-run through
 *   WAAPI on a PAUSED document timeline and sampled. Settled geometry is
 *   measured with transition-duration collapsed to 0s.
 */
/**
 * THE HOMEPAGE.
 *
 * Applied 2026-09-20 from the preview library, where it was built and
 * measured as ab-op1. The page it replaced is kept whole at
 * src/pages/preview/ab-live.astro — byte for byte, with only its import
 * depth changed — so the previous site can be read or restored without
 * going through git.
 *
 * WHAT CHANGED FROM THE PAGE BEFORE IT
 *
 * THE ABOUT RUN IS A SET OF SWISS POSTERS. Each movement's name is set at
 * architectural scale and cropped by the screen, drifting sideways on a
 * view() timeline as it passes. Everything under it hangs off a strict
 * twelve-column grid, flush left, one family, with the accent reserved for
 * the full stops — base.css calls the trailing period the most recognisable
 * mark in the identity, and here every statement ends on one.
 *
 * CONTACT IS THE LAST POSTER rather than a different kind of page. It keeps
 * ContactForm's own composition — the two columns, the sticky left column at
 * min-height 80vh, the hairline down the gutter — and takes the poster's
 * rule, label row and type. It carries no heading: the rule and the
 * 06 / place / range row open it, which is what every poster looks like once
 * its word has panned away. The column split is 5 / 7 rather than a half,
 * because a half is the one proportion a twelve-column grid cannot express,
 * and .divide moves with it.
 *
 * THE CATALOGUE IS VISIBLE THROUGH THE WHOLE PAGE. One fixed pane at
 * z-index 1 — above the bed, below the content — carries the ground for
 * every section, so opacity and blur cannot drift between them and the
 * bottom of the page is covered as well as the middle. It is clear behind
 * the name and dissolves in as the conveyor arrives: the bed goes SOFT
 * before it goes DARK, blur at full by 55 per cent of the range while the
 * ground is still two fifths in, which reads as a lens rather than as a
 * sheet of glass sliding in front of the page.
 *
 * READABILITY IS ARITHMETIC, NOT LUCK. The bed is capped at brightness 0.30,
 * so the brightest pixel a photograph can put on screen is #4C4C4C.
 * Composited under the pane the worst background under any word is
 * rgb(34,34,34): white 15.87:1, --bone-dim 11.13:1, the accent full stop
 * 4.56:1. Everything clears AA; white and bone-dim clear AAA.
 *
 * AND IT HAD TO BE CHEAP TO SCROLL, which is most of why this draft exists
 * rather than the one before it. The blur was originally a backdrop-filter
 * on the pane, which means "blur whatever is behind me right now" — and what
 * is behind it is a bed whose transform is rewritten every frame by --prog,
 * so 4.1 million pixels at 1280x800 on a 2x display were re-blurred from
 * scratch on every frame, for the whole page. It is now a filter on
 * .ms__bed: rasterised once into a texture the compositor reuses, with
 * --prog only transforming the result. The tiles' thirty separate grades
 * went onto the bed with it, one filter instead of thirty. Page-wide
 * filtered surfaces fell from 183 to 155.
 *
 * THE BLUR IS EARNED, NOT GATED. It is off until a device proves it can hold
 * its own refresh rate, measured over about forty frames during the opening
 * — where the blur is 0px by design, so there is a free window to watch and
 * nothing is ever shown and then taken away. The bar is the display's own
 * cadence rather than a millisecond count, so it reads the same on a 60Hz
 * phone and a 120Hz laptop. prefers-reduced-motion skips the probe and the
 * blur entirely; it is a stated preference, not a capability.
 *
 * WHAT THIS REPLACED, AND WHY. A guess in three parts — cores, memory and a
 * 900px width rule — which, measured, pointed the wrong way. The bed is 1.2x
 * the viewport in each axis, so an iPhone 15 Pro blurs 4.34 physical
 * megapixels against a 1512x982 MacBook's 8.55: the phone did half the work
 * and was switched off, the laptop did twice as much and was let through.
 * And no static test could have fixed it. deviceMemory does not exist in
 * Safari, so no iPhone can pass it; what iOS reports for hardwareConcurrency
 * could not be tested from here, and guessing wrong gates every iPhone.
 *
 * WHAT THE PROBE CANNOT KNOW: it measures the page WITHOUT the blur, because
 * that is the state the opening is in. A device comfortable without it could
 * still struggle with it. The 1.35 ratio is set to demand real headroom
 * rather than a bare pass, but that is an inference, not a measurement of
 * the thing itself.
 */
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import OpNav from '../../components/OpNav.astro';
import Still from '../../components/Still.astro';
import ContactForm from '../../components/ContactForm.astro';
import { HERO, PORTRAIT, WHO, PROCESS, ACROSS, CLOSING } from '../../data/about';
import { EMAIL, PHONE, WHATSAPP } from '../../data/contact';
import { composition, numberFor, total, RATIO_NUM } from '../../data/composition';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import Opening from '../../components/Opening.astro';
import { framesFor } from '../../lib/frames';
import { BED_SWAPS, BED_CROP, BED_GRADE, BED_TILES, BED_CELLS } from '../../lib/bed';
import { cardK, type CardMode } from '../../lib/cards';
import { infoRows } from '../../lib/wkinfo';

/* HOW BIG AN UPRIGHT WORK IS IN THE CONVEYOR — draft E · Draw, applied.
   Chosen from five built as real pages and judged in the browser; those pages
   were deleted with the rest of the preview library on 2026-09-05, but every
   mode is kept in src/lib/cards.ts along with the reasoning and the
   measurements. This is the one line that selects between them. */
const CARD_MODE: CardMode = 'draw';

const works = await getCollection('works');
const bySlug = new Map(works.map((w) => [w.id, w]));

/* THE COUNTDOWN'S THREE FRAMES, CHOSEN AND NAMED.

   The opening is the Academy leader: three counts, 3 · 2 · 1, each holding one
   frame from the catalogue behind the numeral. The order below is the order
   they are counted in — first entry behind 3, last behind 1.

   THE FRAME NUMBER IS PART OF THE CHOICE, not an afterthought. Everywhere else
   on the site a work is represented by the first frame in its folder; here
   Special Ops shows its SECOND. So the index is carried explicitly rather than
   assumed, and it is 1-based to match how the frames are named on disk.

   This replaced a slice off the front of the score — every other work, eight of
   them, of which the leader only ever counted the first three. That was the
   right shape for the helix opening this succeeded, which flew all eight; a
   countdown shows three, so three are named. */
/* EACH FRAME IS ALSO AIMED, because the ring is a circle and object-fit alone
   centres the FRAME, not the face in it. Read off the ring at 10% gridlines,
   the three faces sit at these fractions of it:

     3  book-my-yamraj   0.50, 0.49   already centred
     2  taskaree         0.50, 0.455  a little high
     1  special-ops-2    0.47, 0.31   well high, and slightly left

   `zoom` and the shift correct that. A shift alone cannot: two of the three are
   landscape frames in a square ring, so cover fits them by height and there is
   no vertical slack to move into — the zoom is what creates the room, and its
   floor is set by how far off-centre the face is. A face at 0.31 of the height
   can only be centred by discarding everything above 0.62, which is a zoom of
   1/0.62. Shifts are percentages of the ring, applied after the zoom.

   THE ZOOM ALSO HAS TO KEEP THE RING COVERED. The shift can be at most half the
   slack the zoom created, or the frame pulls off an edge and the ground shows
   through. Taskaree ran at 1.12 first, which left its top edge 3.7px inside a
   368px ring — correct, but one rounding from a hairline on a smaller ring;
   1.15 doubles that margin and moves the face by a third of a pixel. The ring
   is always square, so these figures hold at every viewport. */
/* AND EACH FRAME CARRIES ITS OWN BRIGHTNESS, for the same reason it carries
   its own aim: the thing being corrected is a property of that photograph.

   The numeral is white, and what threatens it is not how bright the frame is
   on average but the brightest PATCHES falling inside the glyph's own outline.
   Measured under the real 3/2/1 ink at ring size, the three were unequal and
   two were under the 3:1 floor that text this large wants — the 2 worst at
   2.2:1, on a highlight that belongs to Taskaree alone.

   One global value would have to be dark enough for Taskaree and would then
   take a fifth of Special Ops' brightness away to fix a highlight that is not
   in it. So each frame is dimmed only as far as ITS OWN worst pixel requires,
   which is the brightest each can be:

     3  book-my-yamraj  0.67   2.9:1 -> 4.0:1
     2  taskaree        0.58   2.2:1 -> 4.0:1
     1  special-ops-2   0.76   3.6:1 -> 4.0:1

   Full workings, both viewports and the ring means that show the frames are
   still photographs, are in the filter comment in Opening.astro. Re-measure
   there rather than nudging these by eye — and re-measure if a frame or an aim
   changes, because the aim decides which part of the photograph is under the
   glyph in the first place. */
const COUNTDOWN: { slug: string; frame: number; zoom?: number; x?: number; y?: number; dim: number }[] = [
  { slug: 'book-my-yamraj', frame: 1, dim: 0.67 },                            // behind 3
  { slug: 'taskaree', frame: 1, zoom: 1.15, y: 5, dim: 0.58 },                // behind 2
  { slug: 'special-ops-2', frame: 2, zoom: 1.7, x: 5.4, y: 32.6, dim: 0.76 }, // behind 1
];
const stills = COUNTDOWN.map(({ slug, frame, zoom, x, y, dim }) => {
  const w = bySlug.get(slug)!;
  const all = framesFor(slug, w.data.frames);
  /* Clamped, so a frame that is renumbered or removed degrades to the last one
     the work has rather than failing the build. */
  return {
    img: all[Math.min(Math.max(1, frame), all.length) - 1],
    alt: w.data.alt,
    aim: zoom || x || y ? { zoom: zoom ?? 1, x: x ?? 0, y: y ?? 0 } : undefined,
    dim,
  };
});

const wordsOf = (s: string) => s.split(' ');
const cut = (s: string): [string, string] => {
  const i = s.indexOf(',');
  return i < 0 ? [s, ''] : [s.slice(0, i + 1), s.slice(i + 1).trim()];
};
const [whoSans, whoSerif] = cut(WHO.head);
const LEDGER = Array.from({ length: 12 }, (_, i) => i);
const clauses = (l: string) => l.split(/(?<=\?)\s+/).filter(Boolean);
const asks = (c: string) => c.trim().endsWith('?');

/* The name, taken from the eyebrow rather than retyped. */
const NAME = HERO.eyebrow.split('·')[0].trim();
const NAME_PARTS = NAME.split(' ');

/* The catalogue's real year range, for the corner labels — read off the
   collection so it can never drift from the works themselves. */
const YEARS = [...new Set(works.map((w) => w.data.year))].sort();
const RANGE = `${YEARS[0]} — ${YEARS[YEARS.length - 1]}`;

/* XL flattened HERO.statement to a string and re-wrapped it greedily at 22
   characters, because its caption is uppercase and had to break somewhere the
   data does not. Corners does not re-wrap: it sets one .line per entry in
   HERO.statement, so the break after "A composer working in" is the one the
   data already carries and the accent stays on the word it belongs to rather
   than being matched back out of a flattened string. The re-wrapper, the Word
   type and the accent lookup all go with it. */

const R = 900;
const STEP = 0.5;
let wi = 0;
const wnodes: { w: any; i: number; no: string; x: number }[] = [];
for (const stanza of composition) {
  for (const p of stanza.items) {
    const w = bySlug.get(p.slug)!;
    wnodes.push({
      w, i: wi, no: numberFor(w.id),
      /* Which side this work drifts to once it has gone behind. Taken from
         its column in the score, as the original Conveyor did, so the same
         work always passes on the same side rather than being scattered. */
      x: +((((p.col - 1) + p.span / 2 - 6) / 6)).toFixed(3),
    });
    wi++;
  }
}

/* The bed's cast and its frame corrections live in src/lib/bed.ts. That was
   so the mosaic drafts could render from the same tables as this page rather
   than a second copy; the drafts are gone, and the split stays because those
   tables are content decisions with long arguments attached. */
const bedCast = wnodes.map((n) => {
  const to = BED_SWAPS[n.w.id];
  return to ? { ...n, w: bySlug.get(to)! } : n;
});

/* The catalogue as a bed behind the whole document. It comes round more than
   once on purpose: a wall of work should read as more than you can count.

   BED_CELLS overrides individual cells with a named work AND a named FILE.
   The cycle alone put the same photograph in a partial cell that was already
   fully visible in the centre, fourteen times over; the table breaks those
   repeats by reaching for a different frame rather than a different work. A
   cell it does not name falls through to the cycle exactly as before, which
   is what keeps the twelve centre tiles untouched. */
const GROUND_TILES = Array.from({ length: BED_TILES }, (_, i) => {
  const pick = BED_CELLS[i];
  if (pick) return { w: bySlug.get(pick.slug)!, frames: [pick.frame], set: true };
  const n = bedCast[i % bedCast.length];
  return { w: n.w, frames: n.w.data.frames, set: false };
});

/* D · Poster: splits a statement's trailing full stop off so it can take the
   site's orange. The words themselves are untouched. */
const stopOf = (s: string): [string, string] => (s.endsWith('.') ? [s.slice(0, -1), '.'] : [s, '']);
/* The opening letter, split off so it can be set as a versal. */
const versalOf = (s: string): [string, string] => [s.slice(0, 1), s.slice(1)];
